<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Gear"
    categorySlug="gear"
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    Tested ten laptops for three months. MacBook Air M3 won. Great battery, silent, handles everything. ThinkPad X1
    Carbon if you need Windows. ASUS ZenBook if money's tight.
  </p>

  <p>
    Your laptop runs your remote work life. <a href="/blog/ultimate-home-office-setup">Home office</a>. Coworking space.
    Random cafe. Portability matters more than raw specs. So does battery that lasts through a full cafe session.
  </p>

  <p>
    Quick picks: Air M3 for most people. ThinkPad for business. XPS 13 if you want pretty Windows. Keep reading for all
    eight reviews.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quick-picks">Quick Picks</h2>
  <p>Short on time? Here's the summary:</p>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={quickPicksColumns}
      rows={quickPicksRows}
      caption="Top 3 laptops for remote work and digital nomads"
  />
  <h2 id="why-laptop-matters">Why This Matters</h2>
  <p>
    Remote work asks more of your laptop than office work ever did. Different environments. Long hours. No guaranteed
    outlets.
  </p>

  <h3>Battery life</h3>
  <p>
    Eight hours minimum. <a href="/blog/find-reliable-wifi-anywhere">Cafes don't always have plugs</a>. Airports
    definitely don't. The Air M3 gets fifteen to eighteen
    hours real-world. Leave the charger home.
  </p>

  <h3>Weight</h3>
  <p>
    You'll carry this everywhere. Every ounce counts walking through airports. All our picks stay under three pounds
    with thirteen to fourteen inch screens.
  </p>

  <h3>Video calls</h3>
  <p>
    Constant. You need a <a href="/gear/best-webcams-video-calls">decent webcam</a>, good mics, and a processor that
    doesn't turn your laptop into a jet engine. Bad
    webcam looks unprofessional. Loud fans sound distracted.
  </p>

  <h3>Keyboard</h3>
  <p>
    Hours of typing daily. Cramped mushy keys cause fatigue. ThinkPad has the best keyboard we tested. Hard to
    appreciate until you've used it.
  </p>

  <h3>Durability</h3>
  <p>
    Gets tossed in bags. Temperature changes. Less-than-ideal conditions. Quality build means four to five years instead
    of two to three.
  </p>

  <h2 id="macbook-air-m3">MacBook Air M3</h2>
  <p>
    Benchmark for remote work laptops. M3 chip gives desktop performance with phone battery life. Fanless so it's
    silent.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's special:</strong> Fifteen to eighteen hours battery. Video calls, browser tabs, apps—all silent. Just
    works.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Specs:</strong> 13.6 inch Liquid Retina. M3 chip. Eight or sixteen or twenty-four gig memory. Two-fifty-six
    to two terabyte storage. 2.7 pounds. 1080p webcam. Two Thunderbolt ports.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Good stuff:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Best battery life</li>
    <li>Completely silent</li>
    <li>Excellent display</li>
    <li>Premium build</li>
    <li>Best webcam for calls</li>
    <li>macOS stability</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Bad stuff:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Only two ports</li>
    <li>Base eight gig RAM is tight</li>
    <li>No Windows</li>
    <li>Can't upgrade later</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Price:</strong> Eleven hundred base. Thirteen for more storage. Fifteen for sixteen gig RAM. Amazon often
    has hundred off.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Battery life and quiet work. Nomads and freelancers. Get the sixteen gig upgrade. Worth
    it.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/amazon/macbook-air-m3"
      text="Check MacBook Air M3 Price on Amazon"
      variant="primary"
      program="amazon"
  />

  <h2 id="thinkpad-x1-carbon">ThinkPad X1 Carbon</h2>
  <p>
    Gold standard business laptop. Legendary keyboard. Military-grade durability. Enterprise security. Best Windows
    option.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's special:</strong> Keyboard is exceptional. Deep travel, perfect feedback. MIL-STD tested.
    Spill-resistant. Survives rough travel.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Specs:</strong> Fourteen inch 2.8K OLED or 2K IPS. Intel thirteenth gen. Sixteen or thirty-two gig.
    Two-fifty-six to two terabyte storage. Up to fifteen hours. 2.48 pounds. 1080p webcam. Two Thunderbolt, two USB-A,
    HDMI.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Good stuff:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Best keyboard period</li>
    <li>Lightest at 2.48 pounds</li>
    <li>Military durability cert</li>
    <li>Great ports including USB-A</li>
    <li>Enterprise security</li>
    <li>OLED option</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Bad stuff:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Premium price</li>
    <li>OLED hurts battery</li>
    <li>Intel runs warm</li>
    <li>16:10 not 3:2 aspect</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Price:</strong> Thirteen hundred plus. Frequent sales eleven to fourteen hundred.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Business pros. IT workers. Heavy typists. Best Windows laptop for remote work.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/amazon/thinkpad-x1-carbon"
      text="Check ThinkPad X1 Carbon Price on Amazon"
      variant="primary"
      program="amazon"
  />

  <h2 id="dell-xps-13">Dell XPS 13</h2>
  <p>
    Defined modern ultrabooks. Nearly borderless display. Premium build. OLED option available.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's special:</strong> InfinityEdge display makes 13.4 inch feel bigger. OLED colors are stunning.
    Aluminum and carbon fiber feels premium.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Specs:</strong> 13.4 inch OLED 3.5K or FHD+. Intel Core Ultra. Sixteen or thirty-two gig. Five-twelve to two
    terabyte storage. Up to twelve hours. 2.59 pounds. 1080p webcam. Two Thunderbolt only.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Good stuff:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Stunning display</li>
    <li>Gorgeous OLED</li>
    <li>Premium build</li>
    <li>Good webcam</li>
    <li>Latest Intel</li>
    <li>Compact</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Bad stuff:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Only two USB-C ports</li>
    <li>Keyboard is shallow</li>
    <li>OLED battery shorter</li>
    <li>Runs warm</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Price:</strong> Nine-ninety-nine FHD. Thirteen hundred OLED.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Display quality matters to you. Rivals MacBook looks. Creative professionals.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/amazon/dell-xps-13"
      text="Check Dell XPS 13 Price on Amazon"
      variant="primary"
      program="amazon"
  />

  <h2 id="framework-13">Framework 13</h2>
  <p>
    Radically different approach. Designed for repair and upgrade. Modular ports. User-replaceable everything.
    Sustainable choice.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's special:</strong> Every component replaceable with screwdriver. RAM, SSD, battery, display, keyboard,
    ports. Upgrade motherboard without new laptop.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Specs:</strong> 13.5 inch 2256x1504 or 2.8K. Intel Core Ultra or AMD Ryzen. Up to sixty-four gig DDR5
    upgradeable. Any M.2 SSD. Up to ten hours. 2.87 pounds. 1080p webcam. Four modular slots.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Good stuff:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Fully repairable</li>
    <li>Modular ports</li>
    <li>Excellent 3:2 display</li>
    <li>Strong community</li>
    <li>Sustainable</li>
    <li>AMD option</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Bad stuff:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Battery below competitors</li>
    <li>Build not Apple-tier</li>
    <li>Smaller company</li>
    <li>Cards stick out</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Price:</strong> Eight-fifty DIY. Ten-fifty pre-built.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Tech enthusiasts. Sustainability matters. Want laptop for five to ten years. Developers
    and tinkerers.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/amazon/framework-13"
      text="Check Framework Laptop Price"
      variant="secondary"
      program="amazon"
  />

  <h2 id="asus-zenbook-14">ASUS ZenBook 14</h2>
  <p>
    Premium features at budget price. OLED display under eight hundred. Best value in roundup.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's special:</strong> 2.8K OLED under eight hundred is remarkable. Colors pop. Blacks are true. 120Hz
    refresh. Pay five hundred more elsewhere for same display.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Specs:</strong> Fourteen inch 2.8K OLED 120Hz. Intel Core Ultra or Ryzen 7. Sixteen gig. Five-twelve or one
    terabyte storage. Up to thirteen hours. 2.87 pounds. 1080p webcam. Two USB-C, one USB-A, HDMI, microSD.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Good stuff:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>OLED at this price</li>
    <li>120Hz rare for ultrabooks</li>
    <li>Good ports</li>
    <li>Solid build</li>
    <li>NumberPad on touchpad</li>
    <li>Affordable premium</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Bad stuff:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Average keyboard</li>
    <li>Webcam just okay</li>
    <li>Some bloatware</li>
    <li>Fan noise under load</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Price:</strong> Seven to nine hundred. Frequent sales.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Best for:</strong> Budget-conscious wanting premium display. Creative work. Media consumption. Best value
    twenty-twenty-five.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/amazon/asus-zenbook-14"
      text="Check ZenBook 14 OLED Price on Amazon"
      variant="secondary"
      program="amazon"
  />

  <h2 id="hp-spectre-x360">HP Spectre x360</h2>
  <p>
    Premium 2-in-1. Stunning OLED. 360-degree hinge. Tablet flexibility with laptop productivity.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's special:</strong> Gem-cut design is beautiful. Arguably prettiest Windows laptop. OLED with pen
